1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:05,400
Let’s move now to protection domains

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:10,930
We said that for building a trusted system,
we need to have a clear understanding of what

3
00:00:10,930 --> 00:00:16,939
can be done and who can do it. We can ask
ourselves: what does it mean to have a clear

4
00:00:16,939 --> 00:00:19,909
understanding of what can be done and who
can do it?

5
00:00:19,909 --> 00:00:25,260
To answer such a question, we can note that
each system is composed of a set of objects

6
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:31,839
(could be both hardware and software).
Each object can be manipulated through a set

7
00:00:31,839 --> 00:00:39,020
of operations, and such operations can take
place within the execution of processes.

8
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:47,450
A domain is a set of <object, rights> pairs
that refer to one user or a group of users.

9
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:53,350
According to the Principle of Least Authority,
security works best when each domain has the

10
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:59,710
minimum objects/privileges required to perform
a certain task.

11
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:06,659
Here we have an example with three domains,
each containing some objects and associated

12
00:01:06,659 --> 00:01:12,299
operations. As you can see, the same object
(File 1 for instance) can be associated with

13
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:17,819
different domains and with different operations.
At the same time, the same object (Printer

14
00:01:17,819 --> 00:01:24,780
1) can also exist with the same operation
for multiple domains.

15
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:29,999
We assume that each process is executed in
one protection domain and that the operating

16
00:01:29,999 --> 00:01:36,119
system could allow the process to switch between
domains. In Unix, the domain of a process

17
00:01:36,119 --> 00:01:41,670
is given by the user id and the group id of
the user that is currently logged in and running

18
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:45,829
such a process.

19
00:01:45,829 --> 00:01:51,050
If we now focus our attention on how to maintain
the information about various domains, we

20
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:56,439
realize we need to maintain a matrix like
the one shown in these figures (the one at

21
00:01:56,439 --> 00:02:03,049
the bottom is an extension on the one on the
top with domain-switching permissions too).

22
00:02:03,049 --> 00:02:09,930
In practice, such a matrix would be sparse
(most cells are empty), and storing it as

23
00:02:09,930 --> 00:02:16,270
a matrix is not efficient, we can go for either
rows or columns. If we store information per

24
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:23,380
column , we are then referring to Access Control
Lists. If we do it per-row , we are referring

25
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:27,250
to Capabilities. We will cover both in the
following videos.

